  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1200 PM MST Mon Nov 2 2026\n\nThe potent stream of atmospheric river moisture has begun to\nlift from the region this afternoon. Remnant moisture wrapping\nbehind the front, along with cold air, is producing light rain\nand mountain snow across the region. While precipitation rates\nare expected to remain light, runoff in area streams will\ncontinue today and beyond as the exceptional volume of rainfall\ncontinues to run off the mountains. Numerous flood products\nremain in effect, along with winter highlights for our\nmountains, where snow will get a chance to accumulate this\nafternoon under cooler temperatures. Freezing temperatures in\nthe mountains tonight will lead to some slick travel conditions\non mountain passes, as precipitation begins to taper this\nevening. Snow will continue to taper southwest to northeast\ntonight, with the northern mountains finally drying out sometime\naround daybreak Tuesday.\n\nCooler and drier conditions return Tuesday, with area\ntemperatures running around 5 degrees below normal. Some overdue\nsunshine can be expected across much of the region. Ridging will\nbe short lived, as another disturbance works across the Great\nBasin Wednesday. While moisture remains unimpressive with this\nfeature, a few showers and thunderstorms are expected Wednesday\nafternoon. Given the saturated conditions in place, this\ncommands more attention than otherwise is expected with a\ndisturbance of this stature. The mountains should pick up\nanother couple inches of snow too, as cooler temperatures greet\nthis storm's arrival.\n\nModels continue to sell broad high pressure building across the\nregion to close out the week. This should help streamflows and\nsoils to dry out. Temperatures will remain on the cool side of\nclimatology, but a slight warm up is expected this weekend ahead\nof a potential pattern shift early next week.",
